// Lesson 10.2c — Decks: Shuffle, Draw & Traverse
// rpg jam: implement Shuffle() (the empty method); Fisher-Yates + AP swap.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.2c — Decks: Shuffle, Draw &amp; Traverse</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The starter's <code>Shuffle()</code> is EMPTY — check your copy.
    That emptiness is a gift: the single most exam-famous algorithm
    of the year, left in the seat where you sit. Today you implement
    the shuffle, wire the draw, and traverse a hand to render it.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Fisher-Yates, in exam language.</strong> The AP
    pseudocode for a shuffle (know BOTH languages):</p>
    <pre><code>AP:                                C#:
FOR i FROM LENGTH(list) TO 2     for (int i = list.Count - 1; i &gt; 0; i--)
    r ← RANDOM(1, i)                 int r = Random.Range(0, i + 1);
    SWAP(list[i], list[r])           (card temp = list[i];
                                     list[i] = list[r];
                                     list[r] = temp;)</code></pre>
    <p>Read it as a story: walk the list from the END; at each
    position, pick a random EARLIER-or-equal position; swap. Every
    card gets exactly one chance at every slot — that fairness
    (uniform distribution) is why this is THE textbook shuffle, not
    a vibe shuffle.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The lazy shuffle everyone writes first: repeatedly pick
            a random card and REMOVE it into a new list. It works
            — why is it worse than Fisher-Yates (cost per removal:
            the list shifts every element)? And why does the exam
            phrase "every possible order is equally likely" make
            the lazy version suspicious?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — fill the empty method.</strong> In
    GameManager, implement Shuffle() with the C# above (rename to
    your list's name — read the starter's Deal() first: it is
    PARTIALLY implemented, and it shows you the intended list
    names and the card flow).</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the draw, in the starter's voice:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>public void Draw()
{
    if (deck.Count == 0)
    {
        // reshuffle the discard pile into the deck — the loop of loops
        return;
    }
    Card topCard = deck[0];
    deck.RemoveAt(0);      // AP: REMOVE(deck, 1)  — index 1 in exam-speak!
    hand.Add(topCard);    // AP: APPEND(hand, topCard)
}</code></pre>
    <p>Check the boundary: draw from an EMPTY deck crashes
    (deck[0] does not exist). The guard clause is the whole
    difference between a jam build and a jam crash.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — traverse to render.</strong> A hand is a
    list; showing it means visiting every element. Two traversal
    styles, both in the exam:</p>
    <pre><code>// index loop — you NEED i for positions:
for (int i = 0; i &lt; hand.Count; i++)
{
    SpawnCardVisual(hand[i], i);
}

// foreach — you only need each card:
foreach (Card c in hand) { Debug.Log(c.cardName); }</code></pre>
    <p>Spawn your card prefabs across the screen by index (i sets
    the x position — the hand literally spreads because of a
    loop counter). Why does the index loop earn its extra ceremony
    here? Because position IS the index.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — the AP traverse question, answered by
    your own game:</strong> "sum the cost of all cards in hand" —
    write it in exam pseudocode with your game's real names, then
    prove the answer with a Debug.Log total.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The shuffle bugs</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Random.Range(0, i) instead of (0, i + 1). Which
                card can NEVER move? (The last one! Why — trace
                i's first value.)</li>
            <li>Swapping with a temp VARIABLE FORGOTTEN: list[i] =
                list[r]; list[r] = list[i]; — what does the deck
                become, and why does it LOOK shuffled at a
                glance?</li>
            <li>Removing while traversing: a discard-all loop
                over the hand while removing from it. Which
                cards get SKIPPED — and why does looping backward
                fix it exactly (<a href="10.2a.html">10.2a</a>'s
                warning, now real)?</li>
            <li>Draw from an empty deck: what does deck[0] DO when
                Count is 0? (Try it — read the exception. Then
                thank the guard clause you wrote.)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Close of Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~5 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Shuffle science</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Test the shuffle like a scientist: shuffle a
                3-card deck 60 times, tally the orders. Six
                possible orders — how close to 10 each? (This is
                <a href="9.5a.html">9.5a</a> thinking: a claim
                about randomness, CHECKED.)</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"10.2c: shuffle + draw + render hand"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Implement Fisher-Yates in C# and match it line by line to AP pseudocode</li>
            <li>Draw safely with the empty-deck guard</li>
            <li>Render a hand by index loop (position = index)</li>
            <li>Explain remove-while-traversing and its backward fix</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/lists-arrays.html">Lists &amp; Arrays</a> — shuffles, traversals, the works</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/reference-sheet.html">Exam Reference Sheet</a> — RANDOM, SWAP, traversals</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.2d.html">10.2d — Turn Logic: Selection + Iteration</a></p>
</div>`;
